{"schema":1,"slug":"ojibwe-and-odawa-on-birds-war-road","nation":"Ojibwe (Chippewa) and Odawa","baseRef":"/data/web/territories/_base-great-lakes.json","yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1780,"presenceLabel":"Ojibwe and Odawa war road, June 1780","presenceNote":"Event route only: one campaign (25 May–4 Aug 1780; in Kentucky 21–25 June). The homeland (Lake Superior, Wisconsin, Ontario) is outside this map and is not drawn. The Odawa also have their own story, Egushawa and the Odawa in Kentucky’s War Years, which is a separate page and is not merged here.","confidence":"low-medium","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"The route followed the Great Miami through Shawnee country and the Licking through the northern Kentucky hunting grounds (Henderson). Bird’s force also had Delaware, Wyandot and Shawnee (ExploreKYHistory lists Delaware, Shawnee, Ottawa, Chippewa and Huron).","basis":"Wikipedia (Bird's invasion of Kentucky) for the route and the ~100 Odawa and Ojibwe, ExploreKYHistory #591, Greenville Art. 3 for Loramie's store. Route drawn on base-map river centrelines.","basisChip":"ethnohistory","timeline":{"min":1700,"max":1781},"fadeYears":0,"fadeSouth":false,"view":{"full":"186 135 540 494","narrow":"186 135 540 494","card":"186 135 540 494"},"mapLabelPos":[-83.9,44.3],"pins":[],"startEra":{"ojibwe-on-birds-war-road":"bird"},"stories":{"ojibwe-on-birds-war-road":{"yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1780,"presenceLabel":"Ojibwe and Odawa war road"}},"legend":[{"kind":"soft","label":"Approximate general area of Odawa and Ojibwe presence (Detroit, Saginaw, Maumee; whole counties, not a boundary)"},{"kind":"line","label":"Licking River reach to Falmouth (solid, in Kentucky)"},{"kind":"point","label":"Site (exact position)"},{"kind":"ring","label":"Site, location approximate"}],"eras":[{"id":"home","label":"c.1700–1780 · Detroit-area Odawa and Ojibwe","short":"c.1700","yearStart":1700,"yearEnd":1779,"confidence":"low","sources":["wp_bird","wp_odawa","wp_ojibwe"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"Odawa and Ojibwe (Chippewa) lived in the Detroit, Maumee and Saginaw country and farther north in Michigan and Ontario; with the Potawatomi they are the Three Fires. The tint is an approximate general area of the southeast Michigan and northwest Ohio part of that country, built from whole counties; their wider country to the north is larger and is not drawn. Detroit is the muster point for Bird’s 1780 force.","layers":[{"feature":"oo_area","style":"soft"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area · wider homeland not drawn","mapLabel":"OJIBWE · ODAWA","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1700–1779","mapLabelPos":[-83.9,44.3]},{"id":"bird","label":"25 May–Aug 1780 · Bird’s war road","short":"1780","yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1780,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["wp_bird","ekh591","greenville"],"storySlugs":["ojibwe-on-birds-war-road"],"text":"Bird left Detroit on 25 May 1780 with about 100 Odawa and Ojibwe from the tinted country (approximate general area), went up the Maumee and Auglaize, portaged to the Great Miami and down to the Ohio, joined McKee’s mostly Shawnee warriors, then went up the Licking and took Ruddle’s (22 June) and Martin’s Stations. Only the Kentucky reach (Ohio mouth to Falmouth) is drawn, as a solid line. The Odawa chief Egushawa has a separate story; it is not merged into this page.","layers":[{"feature":"oo_area","style":"soft"},{"feature":"licking_reach","style":"line"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"},{"feature":"martins","style":"point"}],"mapLabel":"OJIBWE · ODAWA","apprTag":"Approximate general area · route solid in Kentucky","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1780 (came from here)","mapLabelPos":[-83.9,44.3]}],"sources":[{"id":"wp_bird","label":"Bird's invasion of Kentucky (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird%27s_invasion_of_Kentucky"},{"id":"ekh591","label":"British, Indian Raid (ExploreKYHistory #591)","url":"https://explorekyhistory.ky.gov/items/show/591"},{"id":"gr